message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'The Determinant''s Free Lunch: Local Symmetry and the Permanent''s Missing Moves'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/the-determinants-free-lunch.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-the-determinants-free-lunch
abstract: 'Agents that each hold one row of an invertible matrix over a finite field, and see only their own row and a neighbour''s, can preserve the determinant without observing it: subtracting multiples of rows and exchanging rows with a sign change are determinant-one maps of two rows. We ask what supplies this capacity, when it suffices and whether the permanent shares it. On a communication graph, determinant-one moves of adjacent pairs generate the full special linear group on each connected component, so the reachable matrices are those sharing each component''s exterior product of rows. Agents can reach a form whose determinant is readable from per-row summaries exactly when the pivot profiles of the components'' row spaces are disjoint, and a randomised local protocol then reaches it with probability one. Exhaustive checks over 1,381,440 matrix-graph pairs and 400 seeded trials over $\mathbb{F}_{101}$ agree with these theorems. For the permanent we prove that the only linear maps of two rows preserving it in every matrix of size at least three are scalings and exchanges, against all of $\mathrm{SL}_2$ for the determinant, so linear permanent-preserving agents never create a zero. Moves that depend on the state remove this rigidity at small sizes: every $3\times 3$ matrix over $\mathbb{F}_5$ with nonzero permanent can reach a readable form, while greedy agents with access to every permanent-preserving move complete 94 of 200 random $6\times 6$ matrices over $\mathbb{F}_3$. The results do not bear on whether VP differs from VNP.'
